- the header date at line 3;
- the line 564 benchmark row, which adds --runtime-concurrency and --runtime-stagger and references RuntimeConcurrencyBenchmark.swift;
- head lines 1208-1209 in the benchmark flags table, whose head text is not visible in these units.

All of these are documentation-only changes to a local, operator-invoked benchmark diagnostic. That diagnostic states that the prefix cache is off. These changes do not alter:
- the coordinator/provider WebSocket contract (TB-002);
- attestation or App Attest (TB-009, TB-011);
- the token lifecycle (A-008, T-053);
- the fan helper (TB-010);
- prefix-cache gating or TTL (T-041, SEC-035).

The surrounding unchanged text documents existing behavior, including:
- LaunchAgent env passthrough, with DARKBLOOM_APP_ATTEST_WORKER_QUALIFICATION accepted only when exactly 1 (line 1752);
- report upload bounds;
- logout and unenroll semantics;
- cache env forwarding (lines 1700-1721).

None of this is a regression introduced here. No regression or new attack surface was identified. The integration pass may want to confirm two things: that the RuntimeConcurrencyBenchmark source keeps prefix caching disabled, and that it is not reachable from the serving path. That source is outside the supplied evidence.
